
{"id":"3eee8dc4-d261-4426-a3a5-9f8b07432eab","object":"chat.completion","created":1785032620,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"import json\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\\n    \\n    # Build node map and children map\\n    node_by_id = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_by_id[nid] = node\\n        children[nid] = []\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children[parent].append(node['id'])\\n    \\n    # Initial queue sorted by descending depth, then ascending id\\n    all_ids = list(node_by_id.keys())\\n    all_ids.sort(key=lambda nid: (-node_by_id[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n    queue = list(all_ids)\\n    \\n    # Track which node IDs have ever been added","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function `solve(case)` that processes the given case payload. The case payload contains a list of nodes with properties: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also utility_threshold. The task: perform a reduction/queue process.\n\nThe description: \"Maintain and update the syntax-guided reduction work queue across accepted and rejected tree rewrites without revisiting invalidated nodes.\" But from the explicit fixture and constraints, it seems we need to simulate a process: initialize queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order. Then process nodes: skip removed nodes. Remove only a removable leaf whose utility is at most threshold. After removal, enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration. Return retained_node_ids (presumably the IDs of nodes that remain after removal process) and visit_order (the order in which nodes were visited/processed?).\n\nLet's parse the instructions carefully:\n\n\"Initialize a queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order.\" So we sort all nodes (including root?) by depth descending, then by id (string) ascending? \"stable node-ID order\" likely means ascending order but tie-breaking by id. So for same depth, sort by id alphabetically or numerically? IDs are strings like \"root\", \"n1\", etc. Probably lexicographic.\n\n\"Skip nodes already removed.\" So when we pop from queue, we check if node is still present.\n\n\"Remove only a removable leaf whose utility is at most the threshold.\" Leaf: node with no children? In the tree structure, we need to compute children from parent links. We have nodes list with parent field. We can build adjacency or children list. Leaf: a node that has no children in the current tree (after removals). Also it must be removable (removable == true) and its utility <= threshold.\n\n\"After removal enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration.\" So if we remove a node, we add its parent (if parent is not None and parent is not removed) to the queue. But careful: the parent might already be in queue? The instruction says \"enqueue\" which could mean add to the queue if not already present? Or we just append? The phrase \"for reconsideration\" suggests we should add it to the queue again even if already present? But typical BFS/DFS might check duplicates. However, the spec says \"enqueue\" without deduplication. But later: \"no_invalidated_revisit\" might imply we should avoid revisiting nodes that have been invalidated? Hmm.\n\n\"Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\" So we need to track the order in which nodes are considered (visited) from the queue? The queue processing order? The initial queue ordering? Then when we process a node (pop from queue), we record its ID in visit_order? But what about nodes that are skipped because removed? The spec says \"Skip nodes already removed.\" So if we pop a node that has been removed, we skip it and still record it? Maybe it's not recorded because it's removed? Need to interpret.\n\nFrom typical reduction algorithms, \"visit order\" is the order in which nodes are processed (evaluated) for possible removal. So each time we pop a node from the queue, we consider it. If it's already removed, we skip without recording? Or record? The phrase \"complete visit order\" suggests every node that was visited (popped) should be recorded, even if skipped.\n\nLet's look at the output schema: \"visit_order\" is an array of strings (node IDs). So we need to output the sequence of node IDs as we process them.\n\nAlso \"retained_node_ids\" is the set of node IDs that remain after all removals (i.e., not removed).\n\nWe need to simulate until queue is empty.\n\nInitial queue: All nodes sorted by depth descending, then node ID ascending.\n\nThen loop: While queue not empty:\n  Pop front (or first element, assuming queue is FIFO? \"queue\" implies FIFO. But the order is important: descending depth then ID. Then after removals, we enqueue parent which may be at a higher depth (shallower) ??? But we are using a queue, not a stack. So it's BFS-like order? Actually \"descending depth\" means deepest first. Then after removal, enqueue parent (shallower). That parent will be enqueued at the end of the queue, so it will be processed later after all other nodes currently in queue (which are deeper or same depth). That seems plausible.\n\nNow, what about nodes that are not removable? We still need to process them? The description: \"Remove only a removable leaf whose utility is at most the threshold.\" That seems to be the only condition for removal. For nodes that are not leaves, or not removable, or utility > threshold, we do nothing? But we still need to record visit? Probably yes, we visit them, but they are not eligible for removal, so they are just skipped. But the algorithm might need to enforce that we only consider nodes from the queue for removal. So each popped node is evaluated: if it is a leaf (currently), removable, and utility <= threshold, then remove it and enqueue its parent. Otherwise, do nothing (node remains). Then continue.\n\nBut note: After removal, the tree changes. A node that was previously a leaf might become a leaf later after its children are removed. So we need to recompute leaf status on the fly? The queue processing order naturally handles that: when we later process a node (e.g., parent after children removed), it might become a leaf and be eligible for removal.\n\nNow, we need to ensure we don't revisit invalidated nodes: \"no_invalidated_revisit\". That might mean that after removing a node, we should not later revisit that node (it's removed). That's handled by skipping removed nodes. Also, when we enqueue a parent for reconsideration, we should not enqueue it if it is already removed? But the parent is retained, so it's not removed. Also, we might enqueue a node multiple times? The spec says \"enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration.\" It doesn't say to deduplicate. However, if we enqueue a node that is already in the queue, it might cause multiple visits. That could be allowed. But then we need to handle that when we pop it, if it's already removed? But the parent is retained, so not removed. However, it could be removed later. So we might visit it multiple times. But the spec says \"no_invalidated_revisit\" but that might refer to not revisiting nodes that are already removed? Or maybe it means we should not revisit nodes that have been \"invalidated\" by a removal? Hmm.\n\nGiven the complexity, I'll assume the standard interpretation: we maintain a queue that initially contains all nodes in descending depth then ID order. We process each node once (when popped). If the node is still present (not removed) and it is a leaf, removable, and utility ≤ threshold, then we remove it and enqueue its parent (if parent exists and parent is not removed). The visit_order is the order in which we pop nodes (including those skipped because removed). For nodes that we skip because removed, we still record them in visit_order? Or maybe we only record nodes that we actually \"visit\" (i.e., consider for removal)? The phrase \"complete visit order\" suggests every node that we attempt to process, even if skipped. But there's ambiguity.\n\nLet's look at the fixture examples to infer expected output. We have three cases. For case b-000: nodes list, utility_threshold=0.55. We need to simulate and see what retained nodes and visit order would be. Then we can infer if our interpretation matches typical expected output.\n\nFirst, I'll manually simulate for case b-000. Nodes:\nroot (depth0, not removable, utility1.0)\nn1 (depth1, parent root, not removable, utility0.941)\nn2 (depth1, parent root, removable, utility0.742)\nn3 (depth1, parent root, not removable, utility0.760)\nn4 (depth2, parent n2, removable, utility0.208)\nn5 (depth2, parent n3, not removable, utility0.021)\nn6 (depth2, parent n1, removable, utility0.352)\n\nThreshold=0.55. So removable leaves with utility<=0.55: n4 (0.208), n6 (0.352). n5 is not removable. n2 utility=0.742 >0.55, not eligible even if leaf, but it's not leaf initially (has child n4). n1 has child n6, utility>0.55, not removable anyway. n3 has child n5, not removable. root not removable.\n\nInitial queue sorted by depth descending (depth2 first), then ID ascending: depth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6 (lexicographic: n4, n5, n6). Then depth1: n1, n2, n3. Then depth0: root.\n\nQueue order: n4, n5, n6, n1, n2, n3, root.\n\nProcess:\n- Pop n4: leaf? Check children: n4 has no children (only n2 is parent, no child of n4). Yes, leaf. removable=True, utility=0.208 ≤0.55 => remove n4. Enqueue its parent n2 (if retained). n2 is in queue later, but we enqueue it now. Add n2 to end of queue. Visit order: [n4].\n- Pop n5: leaf? n5 has no children, removable=False, so not removed. Visit order: [n4, n5].\n- Pop n6: leaf? yes, removable=True, utility=0.352 ≤0.55 => remove n6. Enqueue parent n1. Visit order: [n4, n5, n6].\n- Pop n1: now n1 is leaf? After removing n6, n1 has no children, so it is a leaf. Check: