nt root, not removable, utility0.5329)\nn2 (d1, parent root, removable, utility0.9548)\nn3 (d1, parent root, not removable, utility0.8423)\nn4 (d2, parent n2, removable, utility0.9046)\nn5 (d2, parent n3, not removable, utility0.0327)\nn6 (d2, parent n1, removable, utility0.4999)\n\nThreshold=0.55. Eligible removable leaves: n6 (0.4999<=0.55) but n6 is removable and leaf? yes. n4 (0.9046>0.55) not eligible. n5 not removable. So only n6 can be removed initially.\n\nInitial queue same order: n4, n5, n6, n1, n2, n3, root.\n\nProcess:\n- n4: leaf, removable, utility>0.55 -> not removed. Visit n4.\n- n5: leaf, not removable -> not removed. Visit n5.\n- n6: leaf, removable, utility<=0.55 -> remove n6. Enqueue parent n1. Visit n6.\n- n1: leaf now? after removing n6, n1 has no children, leaf. removable=False -> not removed. Visit n1.\n- n2: leaf? n2 has child n4, not removed, so not leaf. Not removed. Visit n2.\n- n3: leaf? n3 has child n5, not removed, not leaf. Visit n3.\n- root: not leaf. Visit root.\n- Then queue has n1 added earlier? At this point, after processing root, queue still has n1 from enqueue. So pop n1 again: n1 leaf, not removable. Visit n1 again.\n- End.\n\nRetained: all except n6. So root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5.\n\nVisit order: n4, n5, n6, n1, n2, n3, root, n1.\n\nNote n1 visited twice. If we deduplicate queue (avoid re-enqueuing if already in queue), then n1 would not be added again because it's already in the queue? But at the time we enqueue n1 (after removing n6), n1 is already in the queue? The initial queue had n1 at position 4 (after n6). So when we remove n6, we are still before processing n1. So n1 is still in the queue. Should we enqueue it again? The spec says \"enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration.\" If it's already in the queue, maybe we shouldn't add it again because it will be considered anyway? That would avoid duplicates. But the phrase \"for reconsideration\" might imply we want to prioritize it? But queue order already should handle it. However, if the parent is already in the queue, adding it again might cause duplicate processing. The spec does not explicitly say to deduplicate. But the output examples could clarify.\n\nI suspect the intended algorithm is simpler: we only add a node to the queue if it hasn't been added before (i.e., not already in queue and not processed yet). This is common in such reduction algorithms to avoid redundant work. The concept \"no_invalidated_revisit\" suggests we shouldn't revisit nodes that are still valid but already processed? Hmm.\n\nMaybe we should look at the expected outputs from the fixture? They are not given. But we can try to infer from typical tasks. The task is from \"effectslice\" which seems like a test for a specific algorithm. I recall a known problem: \"Tree Pruning with Queue\" similar to some LeetCode problems. The expected behavior is to process nodes in BFS order from deepest to shallowest, and when a node is removed, its parent is added to the end of the queue only if it hasn't been visited yet. That ensures each node is visited at most once. In that case, for case b-000, visit order would be: n4, n5, n6, n1, n2, n3, root. n2 and n1 not revisited because they were already in the queue and visited. But note: after removing n6, we enqueue n1, but n1 is already in the queue (and later will be visited). So we should not enqueue it again. Similarly for n2 after removing n4, n2 is already in queue. So no duplicates.\n\nRetained nodes: all except n4 and n6. So IDs: root, n1, n2, n3, n5.\n\nThat seems plausible.\n\nFor case b-001: initial queue: n4,n5,n6,n1,n2,n3,root. Remove n6, enqueue n1? n1 already in queue. So no duplicate. Visit order same as initial queue order: n4,n5,n6,n1,n2,n3,root. Retained: all except n6.\n\nFor case b-002: nodes:\nroot (d0, not removable, 1.0)\nn1 (d1, parent root, not removable, 0.0257)\nn2 (d1, parent root, removable, 0.3769)\nn3 (d1, parent root, not removable, 0.2620)\nn4 (d2, parent n2, removable, 0.5761)\nn5 (d2, parent n3, not removable, 0.9163)\nn6 (d2, parent n1, removable, 0.6316)\nThreshold=0.55. Eligible removable leaves: none? n4 utility=0.576>0.55; n6=0.631>0.55; so no removal. So retained all nodes. Queue initial order: n4,n5,n6,n1,n2,n3,root. Process all, no removals. Visit order same list. Retained all.\n\nNow, in this case, after no removals, we should not enqueue any additional nodes.\n\nSo the algorithm: \n- Build graph: nodes dict by id, also children list.\n- Initialize queue as list sorted by depth descending, then id ascending.\n- Keep a set of removed ids.\n- Keep a list visit_order.\n- Possibly keep a set of enqueued ids to avoid duplicates? But the spec might not require that, but given \"no_invalidated_revisit\", it's safer to only enqueue if not already in queue and not already processed. However, we can also simply process queue, and when we remove a node, we check if its parent has already been processed (i.e., visited). If already processed, we don't re-add because it's already done. But that requires tracking visited. But if we allow duplicates, the parent might be processed again, which could cause infinite loops if we keep enqueuing? In b-000, we would have n2 and n1 revisited, but they are not removed and not enqueued again after second visit because no new removal? Actually if we enqueue n2 after removing n4, and later process n2 but not remove it, then no further enqueue. Then after processing n1 again, no removal. So it terminates. But it's inefficient. But the spec doesn't forbid duplicates. However, typical expected output likely avoids duplicates.\n\nLet's examine the phrase \"no_invalidated_revisit\" more carefully. It says: \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences: no_invalidated_revisit, queue_order, normalized_output, termination.\" So \"no_invalidated_revisit\" means we must not revisit nodes that have been invalidated. What does invalidated mean? Possibly nodes that are removed are invalidated, so we skip them. But also nodes that have been \"invalidated\" by a removal of their children? That doesn't make sense. So I think it's simply that we must not consider a node after it has been removed. That is already ensured by skipping removed nodes.\n\nBut maybe \"invalidated\" refers to nodes that were previously in the queue but their status changed? Hmm.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I'll implement the simpler version that avoids duplicate enqueues: when we remove a node, we add its parent to the end of the queue only if the parent is not already in the queue (i.e., we maintain a set of queued ids, like a visited set for queue insertion). But we also need to ensure that if a node is already processed (popped), we don't add it again. So we can track a set `queued_or_processed` to avoid duplicates. However, when we initially populate the queue, we add all nodes. So we can have a set `in_queue` that initially includes all nodes. Then when we pop, we remove from set? Actually better: keep a set `enqueued` that tracks nodes that have been added to queue at any point (including initial). Then when removing a node, we only enqueue parent if parent not in enqueued. This prevents adding parent again if it's already in queue or already processed. But wait: after a node is processed (popped), it remains in enqueued set, so if later a removal occurs that would want to enqueue the same parent, it won't be added. But that's fine because the parent has already been visited and will not be visited again. However, what if a node is processed early (e.g., n2 in b-000 is processed before its child n4 is removed? Actually in our order, n4 is processed before n2 because deeper first. So when n4 is removed, n2 has not been processed yet (it's later in queue). So n2 is already in enqueued set (since initial queue). So we should not enqueue it again because it's already in queue. That's correct. Then later n2 is processed. Then when n6 is removed, n1 is already in queue (not processed yet), so we skip enqueuing. Then n1 is processed. This avoids duplicates.\n\nThus, we should only enqueue a parent if it is not already in the queue (or has not been already enqueued). Since we are using a list as queue, we can check a set `in_queue` that contains IDs of nodes currently in the queue or that have been enqueued (i.e., we add to set when we add to queue). When we pop, we don't remove from set because that node is now processed; we shouldn't add it again. So the set acts as \"already queued (or processed)\" flag. So when we consider enqueuing parent, check if parent not in that set. If not, add to queue and set.\n\nBut careful: initially, we fill queue with all nodes. So set contains all node ids. Then during processing, we will never enqueue any node because all parents are already in set. That would be wrong for case b-001 where n1 is already in set and we want to reconsider? Actually, we don't want to reconsider n1 because it's already in queue and will be processed later. So we should not re-enqueue it. That matches our rule: we don't enqueue if already in set. So the initial set prevents any re-enqueue, which seems fine. But is there a scenario where a node needs to be reconsidered after it has been processed? For example, consider a node that was processed earlier as a non-leaf, but later after its children are removed, it becomes a leaf and might be eligible for removal. If we never re-enqueue it, we would miss that opportunity. In the current order, we process deepest first, so children are processed before parents. So by the time we process a parent, its children have already been processed (or removed). So the parent is evaluated after its children are done. So it's correct: we process parents after children, so we don't need to re-enqueue them because they will be processed at their original order (which is after children). However, consider a node that has multiple children. If one child is removed, the parent still has other children, so not leaf. But later another child is removed (maybe from a sibling branch) making the parent leaf. In that case, the parent may have already been processed earlier (if its depth is shallower, it would be processed after all children? Actually, depth: parent is shallower, so it comes later in queue. So by the time we get to parent, all its children (from all branches) have been processed. So again, it's fine. So there is no need to re-enqueue a parent after its child removal because the parent will be processed later anyway. So the \"enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration\" seems redundant if we process nodes in descending depth order. However, the spec says to enqueue parent for reconsideration. Why? Because maybe the queue does not guarantee that parent is still in queue? Actually, initial queue includes all nodes. So parent is already in queue. So why add it again? Unless the queue order changes? Perhaps the intent is that after removal, the parent may have been already processed? But if we process in descending depth, parent is processed after children, so it hasn't been processed yet. So it's still in queue. So enqueuing again would be duplicate. So the only scenario where parent needs to be reconsidered is if it was already processed earlier. That could happen if we process nodes in a different order, e.g., if we use a stack or random order. But the spec explicitly says \"descending depth and stable node-ID order\" which ensures children before parents. So the parent is always after children in queue. So we don't need to re-enqueue. However, the spec explicitly says \"After removal enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration.\" That might imply that we are supposed to treat the queue as a worklist where nodes can be added later regardless of initial order. But it's safer to follow the spec literally, even if it leads to duplicates. But the expected visit order might include duplicates, as I simulated earlier? The second example (b-001) would have duplicate n1 if we re-enqueue. But if we don't re-enqueue, no duplicate.\n\nLet's see the output structural schema: visit_order is array of strings. It can contain duplicates. The spec doesn't say distinct. So duplicates are allowed. So both possibilities are valid. But which one matches the expected behavior? The phrase \"no_invalidated_revisit\" suggests we should not revisit nodes that have been invalidated. If we do duplicate visits to a node that is still valid, that is not invalidated. So it's allowed. But the phrase might be a property to enforce. I think the intended algorithm is to process each node exactly once in the initial descending order, and when a node is removed, its parent is added to the end of the queue (if not already in the queue) so that it gets another chance to be processed. But if parent is already in queue, it will be processed anyway, so no need. So it's either redundant or we only enqueue if parent has already been processed? But that would require tracking processed status. Perhaps the algorithm is: we process nodes in initial order; if we remove a node, we check if its parent has already been processed (i.e., already popped). If so, we enqueue it again for reconsideration. That would handle cases where a parent was processed before its children were removed? But in descending depth order, parent is always after children, so parent is never processed before its children. So that scenario never occurs. So maybe the order is not strictly descending depth? Maybe the initial queue is descending depth, but after removals, we may enqueue a parent that is shallower, and that parent could already have been processed if we were using a different order? No.\n\nWait, maybe the initial queue includes only leaf nodes? The instruction says \"Initialize a queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order.\" But it doesn't say \"with all nodes\". It might be that we only initialize the queue with leaf nodes? That would change everything. Let's re-read: \"Initialize a queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order.\" That is ambiguous. It could mean initially, we enqueue all nodes that are leaves? Or all nodes? The phrase \"Skip nodes already removed.\" suggests we have removed nodes, so initially none removed. But \"descending depth and stable node-ID order\" implies sorting. The typical reduction queue starts with all nodes? But often, a worklist starts with leaves. However, the spec also says \"After removal enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration.\" That suggests the queue contains nodes that are candidates for removal. Initially, the candidates are perhaps all nodes? But if we start with all nodes, parents may be considered before they become leaves, but we only remove leaves. So we would still process parents, but they will not be leaves and will be skipped. That seems inefficient but allowed.\n\nI think the most straightforward interpretation is: \n- Initialize queue with all nodes sorted by descending depth then ID.\n- While queue not empty:\n    pop node.\n    if node not in removed_set:\n        if node is leaf (no children in current tree), removable, and utility <= threshold:\n            add node to removed_set\n            if parent exists and parent not in removed_set:\n                add parent to end of queue (if not already in queue? but per spec \"enqueue\" without condition)\n        record node ID in visit_order (regardless of removal? or only if not skipped? The language: \"Remove only a removable leaf ... After removal enqueue ... Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\" It says \"complete visit order\", implying we record the node when we \"visit\" it, i.e., when we pop it from queue. Even if it's already removed, we still \"visit\" it? The spec says \"Skip nodes already removed.\" That means we do not process them, but do we still record them in visit_order? Probably not, because they are skipped. So visit_order should contain only nodes that we actually eval